# Migration: EventProcessor to .NET 8

## Key Issues with the Original Code

1. **`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** in .NET 8 — it was removed entirely.
2. **`Thread.Interrupt()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** in .NET 8 — also removed.
3. **Race condition**: Multiple `Enqueue` calls between `WaitOne`/`Reset` lose items (only one `Dequeue` per signal).
4. **No graceful shutdown** mechanism — relies on removed APIs.

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
using System.Threading.Channels;

public class EventProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>();
    private Task? _processTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _processTask = Task.Run(() => RunLoopAsync(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task RunLoopAsync(CancellationToken ct)
    {
        try
        {
            await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(ct))
            {
                Handle(item);
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) { }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        _channel.Writer.TryWrite(item);
    }

    public async Task StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        _channel.Writer.Complete();          // signal no more items
        _cts?.CancelAfter(timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5)); // hard deadline

        if (_processTask is not null)
            await _processTask;

        _cts?.Dispose();
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        await StopAsync();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* your logic */ }
}
```

## Migration Pitfalls

| Problem | Detail |
|---------|--------|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`. Use `CancellationToken` instead. |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Also removed in .NET 8. Same fix — cooperative cancellation. |
| Lost signals | Original code: rapid `Enqueue` calls between `WaitOne`→`Reset` only dequeue one item. `Channel<T>` handles this correctly. |
| No manual locking needed | `Channel<T>` is thread-safe internally — eliminates the `lock` + `Queue` pattern. |
| Blocking vs async | `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` burns a thread. `ReadAllAsync` yields it back to the pool. |

## If You Must Stay Synchronous

```csharp
// Synchronous alternative using BlockingCollection (also .NET 8 safe)
public class EventProcessorSync : IDisposable
{
    private readonly BlockingCollection<string> _queue = new();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private Thread? _thread;

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop) { IsBackground = true };
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        try
        {
            foreach (var item in _queue.GetConsumingEnumerable(_cts.Token))
                Handle(item);
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) { }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item) => _queue.Add(item);

    public void Stop()
    {
        _queue.CompleteAdding();              // drain gracefully
        if (!_thread?.Join(5000) ?? true)
            _cts.Cancel();                   // hard stop after timeout
    }

    public void Dispose() { _cts.Dispose(); _queue.Dispose(); }
    private void Handle(string item) { /* your logic */ }
}
```

## Summary

- Replace `Thread.Abort/Interrupt` → `CancellationToken` (non-negotiable in .NET 8)
- Replace `ManualResetEvent` + `Queue` + `lock` → `Channel<T>` or `BlockingCollection<T>`
- Prefer `Channel<T>` for async-first designs (better throughput, no blocked threads)
- Use `BlockingCollection<T>` if you need a dedicated consumer thread
